 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Standard NeRF volume rendering complexity $O(N \cdot R)$, where $N$ is samples per ray and $R$ is number of rays. The depth loss computation adds $O(P)$ where $P$ is the number of pixels, but is negligible compared to rendering.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard NeRF memory usage. Dropout adds negligible overhead.
-*   **Performance:** The configuration is designed for scenes like ScanNet and LLFF. The specific depth loss weight $\lambda$ is tuned per scene (0.007 or 0.02) to balance RGB and depth fidelity [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves geometric accuracy and scale consistency by leveraging depth priors. Dropout prevents overfitting to noisy depth or RGB data.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires ground truth depth maps. Performance may degrade if depth maps are inaccurate or if the uncertainty threshold $\tau$ is not well-calibrated. Reproducing DTU results is difficult due to missing configuration files in the official DS-NeRF code [S2].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+Not given in the sources. The training duration is fixed at 500,000 steps [S1].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+Not given in the sources. The configuration is designed to improve depth accuracy and overall reconstruction quality in depth-guided settings [S1].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** The specific dropout regularization prevents overfitting in the early layers. The small depth scale learning rate ($1e-7$) allows for fine-tuning of depth scaling parameters without destabilizing the main network weights [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The configuration is specific to the ScanNet and LLFF datasets (as per the source details). Reproducing results for other datasets like DTU may require additional architectural tricks not covered in the standard configuration [S2].
+
+**When to use it:**
+Use this configuration when training a NeRF model that requires explicit depth supervision, particularly for indoor scenes (ScanNet) or outdoor scenes (LLFF) where depth priors are available or can be estimated.
 
